# School subjects read off a week's timetable, on Monday I have, and my favourite subject.
lesson language-timetable v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=3 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "My timetable"
  goal "Name school subjects in [[language.name]], say what you have on each day, and say your favourite."

  look {
    scene 23x15 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        table week cols=2 head=["Day", "Lesson"] cells=["[[time.monday]]", "[[subject.maths]]", "[[time.tuesday]]", "[[subject.music]]", "[[time.wednesday]]", "[[subject.science]]", "[[time.thursday]]", "[[subject.art]]", "[[time.friday]]", "[[subject.pe]]"]
      }
    }
    say """
      Leo's week at school: on [[time.monday]] he has [[subject.maths]], on [[time.tuesday]]
      [[subject.music]], on [[time.wednesday]] [[subject.science]], on [[time.thursday]]
      [[subject.art]] and on [[time.friday]] [[subject.pe]]. To say it, put the day first:
      [[subject.on-monday-i-have-maths]], on Monday I have maths. Your favourite is
      [[subject.my-favourite-subject-is-music]]
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.timetable-name k=4
    }
    show language.timetable-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.timetable-meaning k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.timetable-write k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=4
    show language.timetable-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.timetable-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.timetable-write k=5 easy-k=3 hard-k=3
    show language.timetable-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.timetable-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.timetable-write k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.timetable-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.timetable-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[subject.on-monday-i-have-maths]] On Monday I have maths. [[subject.my-favourite-subject-is-music]] My favourite subject is music."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is history and geography taken for each other, and maths and
    science: each naming question describes the lesson and offers the subject most often mixed up.
    The timetable is the same all through the lesson, so the questions about it can be checked
    against one table. The step up from the days of grade two is a day joined to a subject in one
    sentence, and an opinion about it. From this grade the written words are marked with their
    accents: a word typed without its accent is not taken. The dictation is read from this sheet,
    where it prints as Read aloud, or played by the device. If the child is stuck, read their own
    school timetable aloud in the language, one day at a time. Off the page, make the week's
    timetable in the language and pin it up. It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme
    (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "speak in sentences, using familiar vocabulary, phrases
    and basic language structures"; Japan's grades 3 and 4 foreign language activities: "Activities
    to talk about their own thoughts and feelings regarding familiar and simple matters in daily
    life such as times, days of the week and places" (Japan's grade five book We Can! 1 has a unit
    on the week's subjects, recalled); and CEFR A1, "Can understand and use familiar everyday
    expressions and very basic phrases". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[subject.music]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[subject.maths]] and [[subject.science]] and the words to write are [[subject.history]] and [[subject.geography]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[subject.art]] and [[subject.english]] and the words to write are [[subject.music]], [[subject.geography]] and [[subject.history]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
